# 25. Interview Questions and Answers

## Q1. What is data engineering?

> Data engineering is the discipline of designing and operating systems that ingest, store, transform, govern, and serve reliable data for analytics, applications, machine learning, and AI.

## Q2. What is ETL?

> ETL means Extract, Transform, Load. Data is transformed before being loaded into the target system.

## Q3. What is ELT?

> ELT means Extract, Load, Transform. Raw data is loaded first and transformed inside the target warehouse or lakehouse.

## Q4. Why is ELT common in Databricks?

> Databricks provides scalable storage and compute, so teams can preserve raw data and build refined layers inside the lakehouse. This supports reprocessing and multiple downstream use cases.

## Q5. Data lake vs warehouse vs lakehouse?

> A data lake stores diverse raw data flexibly. A warehouse provides structured, governed analytical data. A lakehouse combines lake flexibility with warehouse-style transactions, governance, and analytics.

## Q6. Why Delta Lake instead of Parquet?

> Parquet is a columnar file format. Delta Lake adds a transaction log and table capabilities such as ACID transactions, schema enforcement, time travel, updates, deletes, and merge operations.

## Q7. Explain Bronze, Silver, and Gold.

> Bronze preserves raw source data, Silver contains cleaned and integrated data, and Gold contains use-case-ready data products optimized for analytics, applications, ML, or AI.

## Q8. What is Spark lazy evaluation?

> Spark records transformations as a logical plan and delays execution until an action such as a write, count, or collect is requested. This allows the optimizer to improve the execution plan.

## Q9. What is a shuffle?

> A shuffle redistributes data between executors, commonly for joins, aggregations, distinct operations, and repartitioning. It is expensive because it uses network, serialization, and often disk.

## Q10. Repartition vs coalesce?

> Repartition can increase or decrease partitions and performs redistribution. Coalesce is mainly used to reduce partitions with less movement when possible.

## Q11. What is a broadcast join?

> A broadcast join sends a small lookup table to all executors so the large table does not need to be shuffled. It is useful only when the broadcast side safely fits in executor memory.

## Q12. What is data skew?

> Data skew occurs when a small number of keys contain most of the data, causing a few Spark tasks to run much longer than the rest.

## Q13. What is CDC?

> Change Data Capture captures source inserts, updates, and deletes so downstream tables can be updated incrementally.

## Q14. What is idempotency?

> An idempotent pipeline can be retried without producing duplicate records or repeated side effects.

## Q15. What is Unity Catalog?

> Unity Catalog is Databricks' centralized governance layer for data and AI assets, providing a common namespace, access control, metadata, and lineage.

## Q16. Managed vs external table?

> With a managed table, the platform manages metadata and data lifecycle. With an external table, Databricks manages table metadata while files remain in an externally governed location.

## Q17. What is Auto Loader?

> Auto Loader incrementally discovers and ingests new files arriving in cloud storage, using schema and checkpoint information to support scalable ingestion.

## Q18. Batch vs streaming?

> Batch processes bounded datasets periodically. Streaming processes unbounded input incrementally as data arrives.

## Q19. What is a watermark in streaming?

> A watermark defines how long the engine should wait for late event-time data and helps bound state for aggregations, deduplication, and stream joins.

## Q20. How would you design a data pipeline for RAG?

> I would preserve raw documents, parse and validate them, enrich metadata and permissions, chunk them deterministically, generate versioned embeddings, write curated Delta tables, update the search index incrementally, reconcile index status, and retain lineage from each chunk back to its source document.

## Q21. How do you handle a source schema change?

> Detect and classify the change, validate it against a data contract, quarantine incompatible records, intentionally evolve the target schema when approved, and regression-test downstream tables and consumers.

## Q22. How do you optimize a slow Spark job?

> Inspect the Spark UI and physical plan, identify expensive shuffles, skew, spills, scans, and small files, then filter and select earlier, choose suitable joins, adjust partitions, compact data, avoid unnecessary UDFs, and validate improvements with metrics.

## Q23. Why not call `collect()` on a large DataFrame?

> `collect()` moves all rows to the driver, which can exhaust driver memory and remove the benefit of distributed processing.

## Q24. What is SCD Type 2?

> SCD Type 2 preserves history by adding a new record version with effective dates and a current-record indicator instead of overwriting the old value.

## Q25. ADF vs Databricks Workflows?

> ADF is useful for cross-service orchestration and broad connectors. Databricks Workflows are effective for coordinating Databricks-native notebooks, pipelines, SQL, and compute. The right choice depends on orchestration scope and operational ownership.

